    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "This case goes to a human reviewer, and the SGD 4.00 cancellation fee is held — neither collected from the rider nor refunded — while that review is pending.\n\nWhy the merits favour the driver. The trip record (EV-TRIP-09110) shows the driver's 'arrived' event at 06:50:20 on 10 Sep, and the GPS trace (EV-GPS-09110) places the car about 24 m from the pickup pin — well inside the 50 m STANDARD arrival radius — with its first qualifying in-geofence fix at 06:50:00, twenty seconds before the tap. The car then held position at 0.0 kph in one unbroken 330-second run, 06:50:00 to 06:55:30, against the 300-second STANDARD minimum, and the driver ended the trip at 06:55:30 with reason code RIDER_NO_SHOW, after the wait had elapsed and with no boarding recorded. That satisfies all four limbs of POL-NS-01, so the fee is chargeable, and the SGD 4.00 charge is correct under the fee_schedule resolution rule (the greater of the SGD 4.00 STANDARD zone-class fee and the SGD 4.00 RYDE_X vehicle-tier fee). No rider-side clause displaces it: the driver answered 'Coming down now.' within 20 seconds ('Okay, waiting.' at 06:52:00), so POL-NS-04 does not apply, and the rider's own device sat about 519 m from the pin across all 16 fixes; the arrival marker was not false under POL-NS-05 (24 m distant, 0.0 kph); and there was no waiting-period shortfall for POL-NS-06, the dwell exceeding the minimum by 30 seconds. The missing traffic advisory (EV-ADVIS-09110-01, no payload) only closes the POL-NS-06(i) accumulation route, which this single unbroken run does not need. On those findings the driver's compensation would be SGD 3.20, being 80 percent of the SGD 4.00 fee, and the anti-farming check does not trip: driver_profile.no_show_terminations_30d is 1 (below 4) and 1/120 = 0.008 (below 0.08).\n\nWhy the case still escalates. POL-NS-09(c) is mandatory and terminal. Both profiles record disputes_with_counterparty_90d = 2 (EV-RPROF-09110 and EV-DPROF-09110), and the rider's own filing describes this as 'another dispute with the same driver' — two or more no-show disputes between the same rider and driver in the trailing 90 days. That is a per-se trigger: it does not ask whether any clause resolves the history, and no clause does, so I cannot settle it here. The reviewer should determine whether that repeated rider-driver dispute history warrants a different disposition; the merits analysis above, including the SGD 3.20 compensation that would otherwise be payable, is recorded in the escalation packet. Because escalation replaces the monetary outcome, I make no award to either side, and the clean chat and fare checks do not change that."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

